    out.
  - [1mRules/filters (notify on tag, priority, etc.):[0m most flexible, and the one I'd push back on — it's a query engine
    with a notification system attached, and YAGNI applies hard at this stage.

  [1mMy recommendation: both, implicit default.[0m It's barely more data than the implicit-only version (a subscriber set per
  task rather than a single owner field), and it's the shape a server version would want regardless — so choosing it now
  avoids a migration rather than buying speculative flexibility.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What makes a task one the user "cares about"? → Implicit + explicit follow (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — that's the union shape, so nothing gets migrated later.

  Next: the event vocabulary. This is the part that becomes the stored event schema, so it's worth being deliberate. The
  tension is coverage vs. noise: every event type you include is one more reason for a notification badge to be lit,
  and a badge that's always lit is ignored. Additive, so you can start narrow — but each one you add later means the
  event log has entries that predate it.

  One note on the last option: self-authored changes. If you edit your own task, you generally shouldn't be notified
  about it. I'd filter those out at write time rather than store-and-hide, unless you want the log to double as an audit
  trail.
